    54	- **Certificate** (`ONC\x00`), signed region, exact wire order: `root_doc` (32B ed25519 vk = document ID) · `signer` (32B vk) · `issued_at` (bijou64 seconds) · `hostname` (full DNS name, A-labels, lowercase, no trailing dot) · `heads` (sorted, dedup, count 0 = live) · `predecessor` (an embedded `ONS\x00` successor statement, len 0 = absent) · `signature` (64B ed25519 over fields 0–9). [specs/serialization.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/specs/serialization.md), [design/certificate.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/certificate.md)
    55	- **Attached (unsigned) region**, fields 11–16: `delegation_chain` (verbatim Keyhive `Signed<Delegation>` bytes), `lineage` (`ONR\x00` rotation statements), `chain` (RFC 4034 canonical DNSSEC wire links, root→leaf). This is the single best idea in the repo: "signed = what the signer asserts; attached = what the world can check." Refreshing a lapsed DNSSEC chain, or repairing a chain after generation rotation, needs **no key at all** — anyone can re-attach. Signing them would cause "freshness coupling," break "rotation repair," and freeze evidence. [design/certificate.md §Why the Attachments Are Unsigned](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/certificate.md)
